00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:17.870
as we answer the questions.

00:01:17.870 --> 00:01:20.110
MARTIN OMANDER: Excellent,
so the first question

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:23.710
is from Jacob on Stack Overflow,
specifically about the push

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:25.660
subscription case.

00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:28.390
Jacob writes, "does cloud
Pub/Sub message ordering

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:30.910
mean the subsequent
message is not

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:35.380
delivered until the previous
one is acknowledged?"

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.990
TIANZI CAI: That is correct.

00:01:36.990 --> 00:01:40.060
To use an example from
the ride-hailing app,

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:42.670
if the request ride
message by Bobcat

00:01:42.670 --> 00:01:46.360
is published before the
cancel ride message by Bobcat,

00:01:46.360 --> 00:01:49.060
the request ride message
must be acknowledged

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.040
by your application before
Pub/Sub even delivers

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.950
the cancel ride message.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:55.870
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh,
so does that mean

00:01:55.870 --> 00:01:59.380
that I can create a bottleneck
if my code doesn't acknowledge

00:01:59.380 --> 00:02:00.670
messages?

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:02.560
TIANZI CAI: That is
likely for Bobcat,

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:05.350
but it won't affect
other rider IDs.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.380
For instance, this won't
affect request and cancel

00:02:08.380 --> 00:02:11.009
ride messages from Starfish.

00:02:11.009 --> 00:02:13.320
To avoid blocking
subsequent messages

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.930
from individual
riders, your code

00:02:15.930 --> 00:02:18.450
should always
acknowledge messages.

00:02:18.450 --> 00:02:20.670
If the message is
badly formatted

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:23.340
or if a code throws
an exception,

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:26.040
catch that exception,
acknowledge the message,

00:02:26.040 --> 00:02:28.380
and report the error
to the operator.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.810
Don't fail to acknowledge
an ordered message

00:02:30.810 --> 00:02:33.490
for an extended period of time.

00:02:33.490 --> 00:02:36.150
MARTIN OMANDER: So the second
question is from Robin Thomas,

00:02:36.150 --> 00:02:38.850
in a comment for
our previous video.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:42.630
"What's the behavior when the
publisher sets message ordering

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:45.420
and publishes messages
with an ordering key

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:47.550
and there are multiple
subscribers listening

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:50.280
to the same topic,
but only one of them

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:53.040
is interested in
ordered messages?"

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.780
TIANZI CAI: That is
a great question.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:02:57.990
I think there are
two parts to it.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.870
For serverless and
push, it's easy.

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.560
You have only one push
URL per subscription.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:06.630
For example, in your
ride-hailing app,

00:03:06.630 --> 00:03:10.260
the subscriber is your
service, built in Cloud Run.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.720
Outside serverless,
however, pull

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:15.180
subscriptions are more common.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:18.720
When multiple subscribers,
like multiple Compute Engine

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:22.050
instances, are pulling
from the same topic,

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:24.480
messages of the
same ordering key

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:27.090
are delivered to
the same subscriber.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:30.180
You may hear this described
as the affinity guarantee

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:32.880
of Pub/Sub-ordered delivery.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:35.850
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, so what
if I have multiple subscribers

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:39.030
pulling from a subscription,
and only one of them

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.590
is interested in
ordered messages?

00:03:42.590 --> 00:03:44.510
TIANZI CAI: Subscribers
actually don't

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:49.100
control what kind of messages
to get, ordered or unordered.

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:53.030
Instead, this is determined
by the subscription.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:55.970
If the goal is to separate
unordered messages

00:03:55.970 --> 00:03:58.670
from ordered messages,
it's probably best

00:03:58.670 --> 00:04:01.640
to publish them to
different topics.

00:04:01.640 --> 00:04:04.010
In your ride-hailing app,
it would be a good idea

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:07.520
to publish ordered
messages, like request ride

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:11.930
and cancel ride messages, to
a rider's topic and unordered

00:04:11.930 --> 00:04:15.170
messages, like when a user
updates their password,

00:04:15.170 --> 00:04:17.519
to a different topic.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.380
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, so I think

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:22.260
what this means, to me,
for my ride-hailing app,

00:04:22.260 --> 00:04:25.800
is that, one, if I
use push subscriptions

00:04:25.800 --> 00:04:28.770
and a serverless
subscriber, like Cloud Run,

00:04:28.770 --> 00:04:31.830
I don't have to worry
about multiple subscribers,

00:04:31.830 --> 00:04:34.860
and, two, it makes things
easier if I publish

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.480
ordered and unordered
messages to different topics.

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.760
TIANZI CAI: You got it.

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.480
MARTIN OMANDER: All
right, very good.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:45.300
So now, the third question.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:47.280
Yesterday, in my
ride-hailing app,

00:04:47.280 --> 00:04:49.920
I noticed that I got
an exception when

00:04:49.920 --> 00:04:52.320
